{
  "policy_id": "ref-94-end-the-mineworkers-pension-scandal",
  "party": "Reform UK",
  "policy_title": "End the Mineworkers Pension Scandal",
  "outcome": "O12",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O12",
    "direction": "worsens",
    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "long-term",
    "confidence": "low",
    "plain": "This policy would end the government's 50% share of future Mineworkers' Pension Scheme surpluses, permanently reducing a revenue stream flowing to the Exchequer. The size of the fiscal cost depends entirely on how large future scheme surpluses turn out to be, which is uncertain.",
    "biggest_unknown": "The value of future surpluses foregone by the government \u2014 which drives the entire fiscal cost \u2014 is unknown and depends on scheme investment performance over decades.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy commits to accepting the 2021 BEIS Committee recommendations so that all scheme surpluses accrue to the mineworkers, not partly to the government.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "all scheme surpluses accrue to the Mineworkers"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Under the existing 1994 arrangement, the government receives 50% of surpluses at each valuation.",
        "evidence_id": "E3",
        "quote": "an agreement was made that the government would receive 50% of any surpluses in the scheme's value at subsequent valuations"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The government has received an estimated \u00a34.4 billion from the scheme by 2021 under this arrangement.",
        "evidence_id": "E21",
        "quote": "The government has reportedly received around \u00a34.4 billion from the scheme by 2021"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The 2021 BEIS Committee recommended the government should only receive a surplus share if it first had to fund a deficit \u2014 i.e. the 50% entitlement on pure surpluses would end.",
        "evidence_id": "E18",
        "quote": "the government should only receive a share of surpluses if it first had to provide funds to cover a deficit"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The fiscal effect on O12 is straightforward in direction but uncertain in magnitude. The government currently receives 50% of scheme surpluses under the 1994 privatisation agreement, a stream that has yielded around \u00a34.4bn historically. The policy ends this entitlement on future surpluses (except where the guarantee is triggered by a deficit), permanently reducing government revenue from the scheme. This is an unfunded revenue loss from the Exchequer's perspective \u2014 no offsetting saving or revenue source is identified in the policy text. The Investment Reserve element (\u00a31.5bn) has already been conceded by the current government, so the truly additional fiscal cost is the foregone 50% of future surpluses. Since the scheme has historically run consistent surpluses without triggering the guarantee, the government's contingent liability is unlikely to materialise, meaning the guarantee is largely a theoretical benefit the government retains while losing the revenue upside. That said, the guarantee does provide real value in a tail scenario, so the net fiscal cost is somewhat offset. The magnitude is rated minor because the future surplus stream is scheme-specific, not large in macroeconomic terms, and partially already conceded. Confidence is low because no independent fiscal costing (OBR/IFS) of the future-surplus element is available in the evidence provided.",
    "sources": [
